  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 114 PM CDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nExtreme heat continues to be the story for early September as a\nstrong upper ridge remains entrenched over the Quad State\nregion. Highs in the upper 90s to near 100 and heat indices\naround 103-110 continue each day today through Saturday. Lows in\nthe mid-70s tonight and Friday night provide limited relief and\nhelp surge HeatRisk to level 3 (major) to 4 (extreme) over the \nQuad State region today through Saturday.\n\nWith quick warming this morning, temperatures were lifted\nslightly over much of the area (mainly SEMO), to track closer to\nyesterday's highs. Dew points in the lower 70s have mixed down\nto the upper 60s in some spots, but a little moisture from \nEduardo's remnants will help keep dew points from lowering \nmuch. As we continue through Saturday a few records (highs or\nwarm lows) may be set. This extended period of hot and dry\nconditions will continue to dry out vegetation and increase\npotential for wildfire starts, though light winds will help\nmitigate spread.\n\nNo changes were made to the Extreme Heat Warning during this\nforecast package. There's very limited model support for a few\nshowers Friday afternoon but not enough to keep PoPs out of\nsilent territory for now. A slight chance to chance of precip \nSaturday afternoon in the northeast is accompanied by a weak \ndisturbance rounding the ridge and moving southeastward through \nthe Ohio River Valley. Rainfall amounts should be minimal unless\na thunderstorm can get going. With lower dew points and cooler \ntemperatures Sunday, we will hold off on determining heat \nproduct needs for Sunday. Best potential for heat advisory \nconditions Sunday will be in the southwest and the lowest \nchances are in the northeast. Monday looks to be the coolest and\ndriest day this week with dewpoints into the lower 60s and \ntemperatures only peaking in the lower half of the 90s...which \nis still above normal but we'll take what we can get. \n\nUnfortunately, the ridge builds back in and centers over the\nQuad State midweek next week, with southerly winds bringing in\nheat and humidity. Forecast heat indices remain below advisory\ncriteria for the middle of next week, some of this due to model\nuncertainty on ridge placement, but HeatRisk expands level 3 \nconditions over much of the Quad State midweek. Advisory or not,\nthe above normal conditions are not going away anytime soon.",
